---
// Компонент принимает два обязательных параметра: URL страницы и её уникальный ID (обычно слаг/slug поста)

// Changed 2026-09-18: the import was dropped when the props lost their defaults (see "The
// defaults" below), and is back for a different reason - the Disqus shortname now comes from
// src/config.ts instead of being written into this component. The props are still required.
import { site } from "../config";

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The history of this component, kept next to the code so that a later session does not have to
// re-derive it. DISQUS-FIX.md carries the long form, with the measurements.
//
// 2026-09-17: the comments never appeared on a post.
//
// The callback lived in a block marked is:inline and was written as
//
//   var disqus_config = function (this: DisqusConfig) {
//
// which is TypeScript. Astro copies an is:inline block into the page exactly as written and never
// compiles it, so the browser stopped on that line with "Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token
// 'this'". A syntax error kills the whole block, including the lines below it that load embed.js,
// so the container stayed empty on every post.
//
// For the record, because it decides where such a mistake gets caught: "npm run build" was green,
// since the build never parses such a block, but "npm run check" did report it, as the error
// ts(8010) "Type annotations can only be used in TypeScript files". Measured again on 2026-09-18
// on the pre-fix file taken out of git.
//
// The interface that annotation referred to was
//
//   interface DisqusConfig {
//     page: {
//       url: string;
//       identifier: string;
//     };
//   }
//
// It is not needed any more: the same shape is declared once for the whole project, as
// DisqusPageConfig in src/env.d.ts, where it also types the global that Disqus reads.
//
// 2026-09-18, this rewrite: the block is no longer is:inline.
//
// It is now an ordinary Astro script block: compiled to JavaScript, bundled by Vite, and
// type-checked by "npm run check". The two values reach it through data attributes on the
// container instead of define:vars, which is what Astro documents for this exact case and is the
// shape DisqusLazy.astro already uses. What it buys:
//
//   - "this: DisqusPageConfig" is written in the code itself, and the whole block is type-checked,
//     so a misspelt dataset key becomes an error in the terminal rather than a silent undefined in
//     the browser;
//   - the script is bundled once and cached across posts instead of being repeated inside every
//     page, and comments in it no longer reach the visitor.
//
// 2026-09-18, the defaults: removed, so that the props are required.
//
// They used to be
//
//   const { url = site.social.github, identifier = "my-post" } = Astro.props;
//
// Disqus keys a comment thread by the identifier and the url. A page that rendered this component
// without props would therefore have filed its comments under a thread called "my-post", together
// with every other such page. Nothing reported that, because it is not a build error: it is wrong
// data in a third party's database, and moving a thread afterwards is manual work in the Disqus
// admin.
//
// With the defaults gone, the same mistake is caught by "npm run check" as
//
//   ts(2322): Type '{}' is missing the following properties from type 'Props': url, identifier
//
// measured on 2026-09-18. It is still not caught by "npm run build", which does not type-check at
// all - which is why the check belongs in the workflow before a commit, not only in the editor.
// src/pages/posts/[slug].astro, the only caller, passes both props already.
//
// 2026-09-18, the shortname: moved to src/config.ts.
//
// It used to be written into the embed URL in this file. The colleague had already sketched the
// idea here, in the two lines kept commented out further down, which named a SHORTNAME constant.
// It is one site-wide value rather than a per-page one, so the config is where it belongs: a
// second component needing comments now reads the same value instead of repeating the string.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

export interface Props {
  url: string;
  identifier: string;
}

const { url, identifier } = Astro.props;

// The Disqus site all threads belong to. Site-wide, so it comes from the config; the per-page
// values are the two props above.
const { shortname } = site.disqus;
---

{
  /* Disqus replaces this container with the widget. The script below needs the page URL, the
  identifier and the shortname, and a bundled script cannot take them from define:vars, so all
  three travel on the element as data attributes instead. */
}
<div
  id="disqus_thread"
  class="mt-10 w-full"
  data-disqus-url={url}
  data-disqus-identifier={identifier}
  data-disqus-shortname={shortname}
>
</div>

<script>
  // Step 1. Read the three values back off the container. The attribute names map as
  //   data-disqus-url        -> dataset.disqusUrl
  //   data-disqus-identifier -> dataset.disqusIdentifier
  //   data-disqus-shortname  -> dataset.disqusShortname
  // All three are string | undefined at this point, which is what the check in step 2 is for.
  const thread = document.getElementById("disqus_thread");
  const url = thread?.dataset.disqusUrl;
  const identifier = thread?.dataset.disqusIdentifier;
  const shortname = thread?.dataset.disqusShortname;

  // Step 2. Publish the callback that embed.js reads.
  //
  // It has to sit on window: this block is compiled as a module, and a module has a scope of its
  // own, so a plain declaration would stay private to it. DisqusPageConfig, declared in
  // src/env.d.ts, types both this assignment and the `this` inside the callback: embed.js calls
  // the callback with its own configuration object as `this`, which is also why the callback may
  // not be an arrow function - an arrow function would take `this` from this module instead.
  //
  // The condition also guards against the container being missing, and narrows all three values
  // from string | undefined to string, so nothing below needs a further check.
  if (url && identifier && shortname) {
    window.disqus_config = function (this: DisqusPageConfig) {
      this.page.url = url; // Полный URL страницы
      this.page.identifier = identifier; // Уникальный ID (например, 'my-post')
    };

    // Step 3. Load the widget. embed.js reads the global set above while it starts, so it is
    // appended last, never before step 2.
    //
    // The shortname names the Disqus site and comes from src/config.ts, by way of the data
    // attribute above. The colleague sketched the same idea in the original implementation, in
    // two lines that were commented out there:
    // const SHORTNAME = "biolocation-club";
    // embed.src = "https://" + SHORTNAME + "://";
    const embed = document.createElement("script");
    embed.src = `https://${shortname}.disqus.com/embed.js`;
    embed.setAttribute("data-timestamp", new Date().toString());
    (document.head || document.body).appendChild(embed);
  }
</script>

{
  /* Changed 2026-09-18: the original of the block below was commented out in HTML, which sends it
  to every visitor with the page. It is an Astro comment now, the way the note in Head.astro was
  converted, so it stays in the source and out of the output.

  <noscript>
    Пожалуйста, включите JavaScript для просмотра
    <a href="https://disqus.com">комментариев, работающих на Disqus.</a>
  </noscript> */
}
<noscript
  >Please enable JavaScript to view the <a
    href="https://disqus.com/?ref_noscript">comments powered by Disqus.</a
  ></noscript
>

{
  /* Changed 2026-09-18: the note below was an HTML comment too, and is now an Astro one, for the
  same reason. The task itself is the colleague's and is left to her.

  TODO ? Добавьте #disqus_thread к href-атрибуту в ваших ссылках. Это укажет Disqus, какие ссылки
  искать и возвращать количество комментариев. Например:
  <a href="http://foo.com/bar.html#disqus_thread">Link</a>. */
}
